{#
calendar/_time_grid_shell.html.twig — what a day or a week LOOKS like, with
nothing in it.

Extracted from _view_timegrid.html.twig when the public shared-calendar page
grew week and day views, and for the same reason _month_shell was extracted
before it: the two calendars draw different things in a column — one opens an
editor on an event and can be dragged, the other prints a redacted block that
has no event behind it to open — but the grid itself is the same object in both.
A time gutter on the reader's clock, one equal column per day, an all-day band
that is always there, hour lines, the current-time line, and a block positioned
by fractions of a day.

It is a shell rather than a copy because that list is the visual identity of
this application's calendar. Copied into a second file it drifts on the first
change to either, and the public page ends up looking like a calendar from a
different product — which is exactly the complaint that produced the shared
month grid.

── Nothing session-shaped may be added below ────────────────────────────────

This file is rendered by a page that must not start a session: sharing/'s pages
have their own layout precisely because a `csrf_token()` call sets a cookie on a
URL anybody can fetch. So nothing here may reach for `app.user`, a session, a
CSRF token or the `clock` global — that last one resolves the *signed-in* user's
clock, which is why the hour format arrives as $hourFormat instead. Everything
about editing lives in the caller's blocks, which the sharing side simply does
not fill.

── Positions come from the server, as percentages ───────────────────────────

$grid is a DayGrid per day: the all-day entries lifted out, and the timed ones
each carrying top, height, lane and lanes as fractions of a 24-hour column.
Fractions rather than pixels so the same numbers stay correct at whatever height
the column ends up with, and server-side so the grid is right on the first paint
rather than jumping into place once JavaScript has run — and so a reader with
JavaScript off gets a readable calendar instead of a pile of blocks at midnight.
See DayGridLayout for how the lanes are assigned and what DST does to the axis.

**An INSTANT printed here names its zone; a wall date must not.** Twig's date
filter defaults to whatever zone the environment is set to, and the placements
were computed against the calendar's — an hour label drawn on one clock over
blocks positioned on another is the one failure mode of this view that looks
completely fine, which is why `nowMinute` below is read in $zone. The day keys
are the opposite case and the comment beside them says so: they are wall dates
as strings, and converting one into $zone re-reads it as an instant and can move
it onto the day before. The two rules look contradictory and are the same rule —
format a value in the zone it is already expressed in.

**The all-day band is always drawn, even when it is empty.** Showing it only on
weeks that have one makes the grid jump by a row as you page through, and the
row is a permanent part of the axis — it is where "this event has no time" is
expressible at all.

**Below about 730px a seven-day week pans sideways instead of squeezing.** The
columns used to divide whatever width there was, so on a phone, or in a narrow
docked pane, seven of them came to about fifty pixels each and a block said its
colour and little else. They have a floor of 6rem now: at every width where
seven of those fit the grid is exactly what it always was, and below it the grid
is wider than the scroller, the scroller pans, and a column snaps into place at
a time. Four days are legible in a 400px window where none were.

This was costed here once as disproportionate — "three scroll containers kept in
step by JavaScript, or rewriting the three grids below as one with sticky rows
and a sticky gutter" — and the note on the scroller below is why it is not. The
three grids already share one scroll container, which was done for the alignment
argument there and left this one paid for. All that was left was the gutter: the
first cell of each of the three is `sticky left-0`, and the three pin as one
because one box measures all of them.

The escapes are still there and are still worth having — the pane opens on the
agenda, Day view is one tap away in the toolbar and is a single full-width
column, and the pane's own handle widens it as far as the window allows. They
are choices now rather than the only way to read a narrow week. The shared page
gets the same panning for the same reason.

SPILLING was the other half of this and is also gone, by the floor rather than
by the container query that used to answer it. A heading needs 89px for one line
— measured over the headings below — and 6rem is 96, so the weekday no longer
stacks over the date at any width this grid can produce. The stacked layout and
its query are removed rather than left as a branch that cannot run;
calendar-heading-fit.spec.ts now pins one line at 400px, which is the width it
used to pin two at.

── A column the caller is not publishing ────────────────────────────────────

Same contract as _month_shell, in the same words: a $dayKey with no entry in
$grid is a day the caller is not publishing, which is NOT the same as a day with
nothing on it. The authenticated calendar always supplies every column it draws,
so the branch costs it nothing; the shared page uses it to keep "outside the
window" distinguishable from "free", which is the one thing that page must never
confuse. Such a column is dimmed, marked `data-day-unpublished`, drawn with no
hour lines to read an availability off, and given the caller's own words through
the column_unpublished block.

Embed it, do not include it:

    {% embed 'calendar/_time_grid_shell.html.twig' with { ... } %}
        {% block timed_entry %}…{% endblock %}
    {% endembed %}

Variables:
  dayKeys          list<string>  one per column, Y-m-d, in order
  grid             map           DayGrid keyed by those; a MISSING key means the
                                 column is outside the range the caller is
                                 publishing
  zone             string        the clock the placements were computed on
  today            string        Y-m-d in that zone, or '' for a grid that
                                 should mark no column as today
  hourFormat       string        a PHP date() format for one gutter label
  band             Band          the all-day band over the same days, from
                                 DayGridLayout::band(): one bar per entry,
                                 across the days it covers
  shellOuterClass  string        optional, the frame's own sizing

Blocks. All of them are rendered inside this template, so they read its
variables — `dayKey`, and inside the two entry blocks `entry` and (for a timed
one) `placed`:
  grid_root_attributes    attributes on the outer element: a Stimulus controller
                          and its actions, for a grid that is interactive
  day_heading_actions     the right-hand end of a column heading
  all_day_entry           one bar in the all-day band; `bar` is its BandEntry,
                          and `dayKey` the first day it covers
  timed_entry_attributes  attributes on the positioned block — an event id, a
                          drag target. The style is this file's; anything that
                          identifies what was placed is the caller's
  timed_entry             what is drawn inside the positioned block
  shaded_entry_style      declarations appended to the style of the hours an
                          entry of a day or more shades — its colours, as the
                          custom properties the span-hatch utility reads
  column_unpublished      what an unpublished column says
  grid_footer             siblings after the scroller: hidden triggers, live
                          regions, the forms and dialogs a drag commits through
#}

{% set shellOuterClass = shellOuterClass|default('h-full min-h-0 flex flex-col') %}

{% set nowMinute = 'now'|date('G', zone) * 60 + 'now'|date('i', zone) %}

{# The time gutter, then one equal column per day. minmax(0,1fr) rather than
   1fr: a bare 1fr floors at the content's min-width, so one long untruncated
   title would push a seven-day week wider than the pane and produce a
   horizontal scrollbar over the whole calendar.

   **The width is stated rather than left to the box, and that is what makes a
   narrow week pan instead of squeeze.** `max(100%, gutter + days × 6rem)` is
   100% at every width a column stays readable at and the sum of the floors
   below that, so the grid grows past the scroller and the scroller pans. The
   note at the top of this file is what that buys and what it replaced.

   Written this way rather than as the `minmax(6rem, 1fr)` that says the same
   thing far better, because that does not work here: an `fr` track sized under
   a max-content pass resolves to the widest track's max-content contribution,
   so the moment the box sized itself intrinsically one long title would set the
   width of all seven columns — the exact bug minmax(0,1fr) is here to prevent,
   let back in through the sizing mode. A stated width keeps the fr tracks
   resolving against a definite number, which is the only mode they are honest
   in.

   6rem because a heading wants 89px for one line, measured over the headings
   below. Under that the weekday has to stack, which is what this grid used to
   do and no longer needs to. #}
{% set columnMin = '6rem' %}
{% set columnTemplate = '3.5rem repeat(' ~ dayKeys|length ~ ', minmax(0, 1fr))' %}
{# Where each day is drawn, for the band: a bar is handed over by the day it
   starts on, and only this file knows which column that is — the shared page
   draws days it publishes nothing on. #}
{% set columnOf = {} %}
{% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
    {% set columnOf = columnOf|merge({(dayKey): loop.index0}) %}
{% endfor %}

{% set gridStyle = 'grid-template-columns: ' ~ columnTemplate
    ~ '; width: max(100%, calc(3.5rem + ' ~ dayKeys|length ~ ' * ' ~ columnMin ~ '))' %}

<div class="{{ shellOuterClass }}" {% block grid_root_attributes %}{% endblock %}>
    {# ── One scroll container, three grids ─────────────────────────────────
       The day headings, the all-day band and the hours are all inside the
       scroller, with the first two stuck to its top rather than sitting above
       it as siblings.

       That is the fix for a misalignment nobody could see the cause of: as
       siblings, the two pinned rows were as wide as the pane and the hours were
       as wide as the pane MINUS the scrollbar, so every day column below sat a
       scrollbar's width left of its own heading. Reserving the gutter on the
       other two is not expressible in CSS — `scrollbar-gutter` applies to
       scroll containers and these are not — so the only honest answer is for
       all three to be measured by the same box. Sharing one is also what makes
       a horizontal scroll possible later, which is the open question in the
       note at the top of this file.

       The offsets are a fixed 2rem because a sticky element needs a number:
       `top-0` for the headings, `top-8` for the band under them, and the
       headings are given `h-8` so the two agree.

       `overflow-auto` rather than `overflow-y-auto`: the same box pans sideways
       once the columns reach their floor, which only works because all three
       grids are measured inside it. `snap-proximity` rather than `mandatory`,
       so a deliberate half-column rest is allowed to stay where it was put —
       mandatory drags the view back off every pointer-up near an edge, on a
       grid whose whole interaction is pointer-down and drag. `scroll-pl-14` is
       the gutter's own 3.5rem: without it a snapped column comes to rest UNDER
       the pinned gutter rather than beside it. #}
    {# The hover that lights every piece of one entry together lives here, on
       the one box that holds the band and the hours both. See
       span_link_controller.js. #}
    <div class="flex-1 min-h-0 overflow-auto snap-x snap-proximity scroll-pl-14"
         data-calendar--time-grid-target="scroller"
         data-controller="calendar--span-link"
         data-action="pointerover->calendar--span-link#enter pointerout->calendar--span-link#leave">

    {# ── The day headings ──────────────────────────────────────────────────

       **One line, always, because the column now has a floor.** A heading
       wants 89 of its column's pixels, measured at 10px uppercase: 16 of
       padding, 25 for the widest abbreviation ("MON"), 6 of gap, 16 for a
       two-digit date, and 26 more for the + button and its inset. The columns
       are floored at 6rem where the template is built, which is 96, so it fits
       at every width this grid can produce.

       That is a replacement for a container query, not an excuse for never
       having had one. Seven columns used to divide whatever the pane had —
       about 45px at 400 — and the heading did what a flex row does when it is
       too small: it shrank what it could to min-content and spilled the rest,
       drawing Monday's + over TUESDAY's heading, where it still added an event
       to Monday. The answer was to stack the weekday over the date below 5rem
       of column, on a query per cell, because a column's width is the pane's
       divided by however many days are drawn and no viewport breakpoint can
       tell Day view from Week apart. It worked. The floor makes it unreachable
       — 96 never queries below 80 — so it is gone rather than kept as a branch
       that cannot run, and calendar-heading-fit.spec.ts pins what replaced it.

       `h-8` is not free to change: the all-day band below is stuck to `top-8`
       and the two numbers have to agree, which is the whole point of the note
       on the scroller above. #}
    <div class="sticky top-0 z-20 h-8 grid border-b border-line bg-surface" style="{{ gridStyle }}">
        {# The pinned corner, and `z-10` on it is not decoration: the day cells
           beside it are `relative` and come after it in source, so with no
           number of its own the cell that is supposed to cover them is painted
           over by them instead. Inside the heading row's own stacking context,
           so 10 here is nowhere near the 10 on the all-day band. #}
        <div class="sticky left-0 z-10 border-r border-line bg-surface" aria-hidden="true"></div>

        {% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
            {% set isOutside = grid[dayKey] is not defined %}
            {% set isToday = dayKey == today and not isOutside %}

            <div class="group/day relative border-r border-line
                        {{ isOutside ? 'bg-sunken/20' : (isToday ? 'bg-accent/5' : '') }}"
                 data-calendar--time-grid-target="heading">
                <div class="h-full flex items-center gap-1.5 px-2">
                    {# No zone on a day key, deliberately. $dayKey is a wall date as
                       a string, so Twig parses it in its default zone and printing
                       it in that same zone is the only reading that cannot move it.
                       Converting it to $zone instead re-reads midnight-in-the-
                       default as an instant, which for a calendar west of the
                       default lands on the day before and numbers the whole header
                       row wrong. $zone is for instants — see `nowMinute` above,
                       which is one. #}
                    <span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wide
                                 {{ isToday ? 'text-accent' : 'text-ink-faint' }}">
                        {{ dayKey|intl_date('EEE', false) }}
                    </span>

                    {% if isToday %}
                        {# `shrink-0` is what keeps the circle a circle, and it is
                           half of the answer to "the background isn't round any
                           more". A flex item shrinks to its own min-content
                           before its container overflows, so in a column too
                           narrow for the heading this one was squeezed to the
                           width of the digits inside it — a 20px ring drawn
                           14px wide, which is an ellipse.

                           The other half was `-my-0.5`, gone now along with the
                           baseline alignment that called for it. The row lines
                           its items up on CENTRES: a filled disc has no baseline
                           worth aligning a 10px label against, and
                           `items-baseline` put this one's box 2px above the top
                           of the heading row — which is also the top of the
                           scroller, so the scroll container clipped 2px off the
                           ring and flattened it. Centring costs the text less
                           than a pixel — a 10px label beside a 14px date moves
                           0.8px — and it is the only rule the row needs now
                           that there is one layout rather than two. #}
                        <span class="w-5 h-5 shrink-0 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full
                                     bg-accent text-accent-ink text-xs font-semibold tabular-nums">
                            {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                        </span>
                    {% else %}
                        <span class="shrink-0 text-sm tabular-nums {{ isOutside ? 'text-ink-faint' : 'text-ink-muted' }}">
                            {{ dayKey|date('j') }}
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}

                    {# One add affordance per day, in the heading rather than 24 of
                       them down the column: a tab stop per hour per day is 168 of
                       them in a week, which makes the grid unusable by keyboard.
                       Suppressed on an unpublished column, which has nothing to add
                       to — and is never reached at all by a caller with no editor. #}
                    {% if not isOutside %}
                        {# Positioned rather than pushed there by `ml-auto`, and that
                           is a correctness fix before it is a cosmetic one. In flow
                           it reserved 20px of a 45px column and then overflowed
                           anyway, so the + for Monday was painted over Tuesday's
                           heading and took Tuesday's pointer with it — a button that
                           adds an event to a day it is not drawn on. Out of flow it
                           cannot leave its own column, and the heading keeps the
                           whole width for the date. #}
                        <span class="absolute right-1.5 top-1/2 -translate-y-1/2">{% block day_heading_actions %}{% endblock %}</span>
                    {% endif %}
                </div>
            </div>
        {% endfor %}
    </div>

    {# ── All day ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
    {# **The band's own vertical scroll sits one box in from the grid, and it
       has to.** `overflow-y: auto` makes an element a scroll container in BOTH
       axes — a `visible` overflow computes to `auto` as soon as the other axis
       is not visible — so with the scroll on the band itself, the "all day"
       label inside it resolved `sticky left-0` against the band rather than
       against the scroller that actually pans, and slid out of the gutter lane
       the moment the week was panned. The label stays a direct child of the
       grid; the day cells move into a nested grid that owns the scrolling.

       That nested grid divides the tracks it spans the same way the outer one
       does, off the same definite width, so the two agree to the pixel. The one
       case they do not is a band tall enough to scroll — four all-day events on
       one day — where a classic scrollbar takes width from the nested grid and
       not from the hours below it, and the day columns sit a scrollbar's width
       left of their own. Left alone: it did the same before the nesting, for
       the same reason, and reserving the gutter on all three is the thing the
       note on the scroller says CSS cannot express. #}
    <div class="sticky top-8 z-10 grid border-b border-line bg-surface"
         style="{{ gridStyle }}">
        <div class="sticky left-0 z-10 bg-surface border-r border-line px-2 py-1
                    text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wide text-ink-faint text-right">
            {{ 'calendar.grid.all_day'|trans }}
        </div>

        {# `overflow-x-hidden` is stated rather than left to compute. An
           element with `overflow-y: auto` is a scroll container in BOTH axes,
           so an all-day chip opened wider than its bar would have given this a
           horizontal scrollbar — a second one, out of step with the scroller
           that actually pans the week. Hidden clips it at the far edge of the
           last day instead, which is the only place it has anything to clip. #}
        <div class="max-h-24 overflow-y-auto overflow-x-hidden" style="grid-column: 2 / -1">
            {# **One bar per entry, across the days it covers.** This was a
               column of chips per day, so a three-day offsite said "Offsite"
               three times and read as three events. It is laid out once, by
               DayGridLayout::band(), with a timed entry of a day or more among
               the bars — its hours are shaded in the grid below rather than
               drawn there as a block per day.

               The rows are the band's lanes, and the days are drawn BEHIND the
               bars as items spanning every row, so the ruling and today's tint
               run the band's full height however many rows it grew. That is
               why the rows are stated rather than left implicit: `1 / -1` spans
               the explicit rows and nothing else.

               No `@container` on any of it, as before: the two steps a chip
               would query for are under 7rem and a day is floored at 6rem, and
               a container would make each bar a stacking context that traps an
               opened chip's z-index inside it. #}
            <div class="grid py-1 gap-y-0.5"
                 style="grid-template-columns: repeat({{ dayKeys|length }}, minmax(0, 1fr));
                        grid-template-rows: repeat({{ max(1, band.lanes) }}, minmax(1.25rem, auto))">
                {% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
                    {% set isOutside = grid[dayKey] is not defined %}

                    {# `-my-1` carries the ruling through the band's padding, so
                       a line meets the edges rather than stopping 4px short. #}
                    <div class="-my-1 border-r border-line
                                {{ isOutside ? 'bg-sunken/20' : (dayKey == today ? 'bg-accent/5' : '') }}"
                         style="grid-column: {{ loop.index }}; grid-row: 1 / -1"
                         aria-hidden="true"></div>
                {% endfor %}

                {% for bar in band.entries %}
                    {% if columnOf[bar.firstDay] is defined %}
                        {% set entry = bar.entry %}
                        {# The chip's own continuation rule reads this: a bar
                           whose entry began before the first drawn day prints
                           no start time, because it did not start there. #}
                        {% set dayKey = bar.firstDay %}

                        <div class="mx-1 min-w-0"
                             style="grid-column: {{ columnOf[bar.firstDay] + 1 }} / span {{ bar.days }};
                                    grid-row: {{ bar.lane + 1 }}"
                             data-span-key="{{ bar.key }}">
                            {% block all_day_entry %}{% endblock %}
                        </div>
                    {% endif %}
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    {# ── The hours ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── #}
        <div class="grid" style="{{ gridStyle }}"
             data-calendar--time-grid-target="hours">

            {# The gutter's labels sit ON the hour line rather than inside the
               row below it, which is why they are nudged up by half their own
               height — a "10:00" printed at the top of the 10:00 row appears to
               label the space between 10 and 11.

               Formatted through $hourFormat rather than printed as digits, so
               the axis reads "9 am" for anyone on a twelve-hour clock. The date
               it is formatted FROM is arbitrary and must be — the label says
               which hour, not which day — so it is built at midnight UTC and
               read back in UTC, which is what `false` does here. Handing it the
               reader's zone instead would shift every label by their offset and
               produce a gutter starting at 02:00.

               `z-[5]`, and the number is chosen from both directions: above the
               day columns beside it, which are `relative` and come later in
               source and would otherwise be painted over the cell pinning them
               back, and below the all-day band and the headings at 10 and 20,
               which pin ACROSS it. A gutter at 10 draws its hour labels over
               the band it is supposed to scroll under. #}
            <div class="sticky left-0 z-[5] bg-surface border-r border-line">
                {% for hour in 0..23 %}
                    <div class="relative h-12">
                        {# Midnight is labelled like every other hour, and is
                           positioned differently because it is the only one
                           whose line is the top edge of the grid.

                           The other twenty-three are nudged up by half their own
                           height so they sit ON their hour line rather than
                           inside the row beneath it. Doing that to 00:00 puts
                           half the text above the scroller's first pixel, where
                           it is clipped — which is why the label used to be
                           skipped outright, leaving a gutter that ran 01:00 to
                           23:00 and an unlabelled first row that every reader
                           has to count to identify. Anchored just inside its own
                           row instead, it is the one label that reads as the top
                           of a row rather than as a line, which is exactly what
                           midnight is here. #}
                        <span class="absolute right-1.5 text-[10px] tabular-nums text-ink-faint
                                     {{ 0 == hour ? 'top-0.5' : '-top-1.5' }}">
                            {{ ('2000-01-01 %02d:00:00'|format(hour))|date(hourFormat, false) }}
                        </span>
                    </div>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>

            {% for dayKey in dayKeys %}
                {% set isOutside = grid[dayKey] is not defined %}
                {% set isToday = dayKey == today and not isOutside %}

                {# `snap-start` here and nowhere else: the three grids stack
                   one snap position per day, and one set of targets is enough
                   for all of them because they share the scroller's x axis. #}
                <div class="relative snap-start border-r border-line
                            {{ isOutside ? 'bg-sunken/20' : (isToday ? 'bg-accent/5' : '') }}"
                     data-day="{{ dayKey }}"
                     {% if isOutside %}data-day-unpublished{% endif %}
                     {% if not isOutside %}data-calendar--time-grid-target="column"{% endif %}>

                    {# No hour lines on an unpublished column, and that is not
                       decoration either: twenty-four ruled empty rows are what
                       a reader reads an availability off, and this column
                       publishes none. It gets the caller's words instead,
                       stuck to the top of the scroller so they are on screen
                       wherever the reader has scrolled to. #}
                    {% if isOutside %}
                        <div class="sticky top-16 flex justify-center px-1 py-2">
                            {% block column_unpublished %}{% endblock %}
                        </div>
                    {% else %}
                        {% for hour in 0..23 %}
                            <div class="h-12 border-b border-line/50" aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        {% endfor %}
                    {% endif %}

                    {% if isToday %}
                        {# Rendered at the minute the page was built and never
                           moved afterwards. A timer that crept it down the
                           column would be a repaint a minute forever, on a view
                           that is re-rendered by every navigation anyway. #}
                        {# `z-[3]`: over the blocks, which carry no number of
                           their own, and UNDER the pinned gutter at 5. Its dot
                           hangs 4px into the column to its left, which is the
                           gutter's lane for whichever day is panned under it,
                           and at 20 that dot was drawn on top of the gutter as
                           a red mark floating in the hours.

                           A target as well, because the opening scroll is
                           measured from it — see the controller. The line
                           carries the minute as a percentage already, so there
                           is no second spelling of "now" to keep in step. #}
                        <div class="absolute left-0 right-0 h-px bg-danger pointer-events-none z-[3]"
                             style="top: {{ (nowMinute * 100 / 1440)|round(4) }}%"
                             data-calendar--time-grid-target="nowLine"
                             aria-hidden="true">
                            <span class="absolute -left-1 -top-1 w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-danger"></span>
                        </div>
                    {% endif %}

                    {# The blocks sit in their own layer over the hour lines, so
                       the lines stay visible between and behind them. #}
                    <div class="absolute inset-0">
                        {# The hours an entry of a day or more takes on this
                           day: shaded, wordless, and drawn FIRST so every
                           block of the day sits on top of them at full width —
                           the entry's words are on its bar in the band. The
                           edge that is the entry's real start or end is ruled;
                           an edge that is only midnight is not.

                           Pointing at the shading lights the bar, and the bar
                           lights the shading. It is inert otherwise: a double-
                           click on it creates an event at that time, as on an
                           empty hour, because nothing but a block stops that —
                           see the controller's createAt(). #}
                        {% for shade in isOutside ? [] : grid[dayKey].shaded %}
                            {% set entry = shade.entry %}

                            <div class="span-hatch absolute inset-x-0
                                        {{ shade.continuesBefore ? '' : 'border-t-2' }}
                                        {{ shade.continuesAfter ? '' : 'border-b-2' }}"
                                 style="top: {{ (shade.top * 100)|round(4) }}%;
                                        height: {{ (shade.height * 100)|round(4) }}%;
                                        {% block shaded_entry_style %}{% endblock %}"
                                 data-span-key="{{ shade.key }}"
                                 aria-hidden="true"></div>
                        {% endfor %}

                        {% for placed in isOutside ? [] : grid[dayKey].timed %}
                            {% set entry = placed.entry %}

                            {# min-height is the floor a short meeting needs to
                               be readable. A quarter of an hour is 1/96th of
                               the column — about twelve pixels at the height
                               this grid usually gets — so a fifteen-minute
                               event drew a sliver with its own label spilling
                               out of it.

                               2.375rem, measured rather than chosen: the chip
                               is two rows of `leading-4` now, a time over a
                               title, which is 32px, plus 4 of its own padding
                               and the 2 of `p-px` here. It was 1.75rem and the
                               same number as the all-day lane's floor below, on
                               the reasoning that there was one answer to "the
                               smallest a chip may be drawn". There are two now
                               and there have to be: a lane entry is one line by
                               definition and a block is two, so one number
                               would either clip the block or make the lane a
                               third taller than anything in it.

                               The floored block is then taller than its
                               duration and can reach over the one after it.
                               That is the right trade and not a compromise: the
                               block's TOP is where the meeting starts and stays
                               honest, only the padded bottom is a fiction, and
                               the alternative — reflowing so short events push
                               their neighbours down — would move a 10:00
                               meeting off the 10:00 line, which is the one
                               thing a time grid exists to get right. The
                               overlap is drawn under the later block, because
                               they are emitted in start order and the fiction
                               is what gets covered.

                               Presentational only. A drag reads the caller's own
                               data-starts-at and data-ends-at, never the
                               rendered box, so a floored block resizes from its
                               real duration. #}
                            {# `@container` so the chip inside can answer "is
                               there room for the time as well as the title" —
                               and the BLOCK is the right box to ask, not the
                               column: two overlapping meetings split a column
                               into lanes, so a chip in a 96px week column may
                               have 48 or 32 of them. #}
                            {# A key only on a block cut at midnight: its other
                               half is in the next column, and pointing at one
                               lifts both. A block that is all of its meeting has
                               nothing to be lit with. #}
                            <div class="@container group/chip absolute p-px hover:z-30 lit:z-30"
                                 {% if placed.continuesBefore or placed.continuesAfter %}data-span-key="{{ placed.key }}"{% endif %}
                                 style="top: {{ (placed.top * 100)|round(4) }}%;
                                        height: {{ (placed.height * 100)|round(4) }}%;
                                        min-height: 2.375rem;
                                        left: {{ (placed.lane * 100 / placed.lanes)|round(4) }}%;
                                        width: {{ (100 / placed.lanes)|round(4) }}%;"
                                 {% block timed_entry_attributes %}{% endblock %}
                            >
                                {% block timed_entry %}{% endblock %}

                                {% if placed.continuesBefore %}
                                    <span class="absolute top-0 inset-x-0 h-0.5 bg-ink-faint/40 pointer-events-none"
                                          title="{{ 'calendar.grid.continues_before'|trans }}"></span>
                                {% endif %}

                                {% if placed.continuesAfter %}
                                    <span class="absolute bottom-0 inset-x-0 h-0.5 bg-ink-faint/40 pointer-events-none"
                                          title="{{ 'calendar.grid.continues_after'|trans }}"></span>
                                {% endif %}
                            </div>
                        {% endfor %}
                    </div>
                </div>
            {% endfor %}
        </div>
    </div>{# /scroller #}

    {% block grid_footer %}{% endblock %}
</div>
